{"slug":"/students/subjects/physics/fluids/surface-tension/","source":"students/subjects/physics/fluids/surface-tension.html","area":"students","kind":"content","title":"Surface Tension | Physics","description":"","subject":"physics","body":"<div class=\"max-w-4xl mx-auto\">\n    <nav class=\"mb-6\"><a href=\"/students/subjects/physics/fluids/\" class=\"text-blue-600\">← Back to Fluids</a></nav>\n    <article class=\"content-card rounded-xl p-8\">\n      <h1 class=\"text-2xl font-bold mb-2\">Surface Tension</h1>\n      <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">Surface tension arises from cohesive forces between liquid molecules; causes droplets to form and objects to float if they don't break the surface.</p>\n\n      <section class=\"mb-4\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Examples</h2>\n        <ul class=\"list-disc list-inside\">\n          <li>Capillary rise in a thin tube: h = 2γ cosθ / (ρ g r).</li>\n          <li>Water striders walking on water due to surface tension.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"mb-4\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Practice</h2>\n        <ol class=\"list-decimal list-inside\">\n          <li>Compute capillary rise for water (γ≈0.072 N/m) in a 1 mm radius tube (assume cosθ=1).</li>\n        </ol>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Solutions (brief)</h2>\n        <ol class=\"list-decimal list-inside\">\n          <li>h = 2×0.072/(1000×9.81×0.001) ≈ 14.7 mm.</li>\n        </ol>\n      </section>\n    </article>\n  </div>\n<!-- Topic Quiz System -->","bodyClass":"min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 p-6","bodyStyle":"","css":["body{font-family:Inter, sans-serif} .content-card{-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(16px);backdrop-filter:blur(16px);background:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);box-shadow:0 8px 32px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);}"],"quiz":null,"tracker":null,"links":[]}